 rates to decline within that period","lead":"If PWLB rates do not fall as forecast and the council must refinance in 5 years at higher rates, it will have foregone the opportunity to lock in current rates. This is a material interest-rate risk for both the lender and council.","iso":"2026-07-15","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"HRA financing cost ratio deteriorated to 11.30% (actual) from 10.42% (budgeted), showing worsening debt burden relative to revenue","lead":"Rising financing cost ratios signal increasing debt burden or shrinking revenue, both red flags for affordability. The HRA's ability to service its self-financing debt is directly tied to this ratio; deterioration limits flexibility and absorbs revenue\u2026","iso":"2026-07-15","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","headline":"Gross interest earned on investments fell to \u00a3545k from \u00a3607k despite investment balances quadrupling to \u00a312.4m","detail":"Total investments grew from \u00a33.9m to \u00a312.4m (218% increase), but interest earned actually declined by \u00a362k. The council earned 3.90% on investments, marginally below the 3.94% benchmark, reflecting a compressed rate environment that limits the yield benefit of holding larger balances.","category":"income","matters":"As investment rates compress, the same cash balance generates less income, reducing financial flexibility. This constrains the council's ability to fund activities from investment returns and may force reliance on other sources or spending discipline.","figures":"Interest earned: \u00a3607,187 (2024/25) vs \u00a3544,584 (2025/26); investment portfolio \u00a33.9m to \u00a312.4m","quote":"Gross interest earned 607,187 544,584","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","headline":"The \u00a310m March 2026 borrowing was structured for 5 years because the council is forecasting PWLB longer-term rates to decline within that period","detail":"Rather than locking in longer-term borrowing at current elevated rates, the council chose 5-year terms betting on rate falls. The council states: 'The additional loans in 2025/26 were borrowed over 5 years due to the forecast PWLB longer-term rate reducing within 5 years.' This is an explicit interest-rate bet.","category":"borrowing","matters":"If PWLB rates do not fall as forecast and the council must refinance in 5 years at higher rates, it will have foregone the opportunity to lock in current rates. This is a material interest-rate risk for both the lender and council.","figures":"\u00a310m borrowed for 5-year terms in March 2026","quote":"The additional loans in 2025/26 were borrowed over 5 years due to the forecast PWLB longer-term rate reducing within 5 years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","headline":"HRA financing cost ratio deteriorated to 11.30% (actual) from 10.42% (budgeted), showing worsening debt burden relative to revenue","detail":"The ratio of HRA interest costs to net HRA revenue came in at 11.30% actual against a budget of 10.42%\u2014an 88 basis point deterioration. This measure shows the interest cost is consuming a growing share of HRA revenue, driven by \u00a33.066m annual interest payments on debt.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rising financing cost ratios signal increasing debt burden or shrinking revenue, both red flags for affordability. The HRA's ability to service its self-financing debt is directly tied to this ratio; deterioration limits flexibility and absorbs revenue that could fund service improvements or reserves.","figures":"HRA financing costs: 10.42% budgeted, 11.30% actual; annual HRA interest payments \u00a33.066m","quote":"Ratio of financing costs to net revenue stream (note 2)... HRA (applies only to housing authorities) 10.57% 10.42% 11.30%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","headline":"Council drew \u00a310m additional PWLB borrowing in March 2026 to fund net unfinanced capital expenditure","detail":"In March 2026, the council borrowed \u00a310m from the Public Works Loan Board (\u00a35m HRA, \u00a35m GRA) to cover unfinanced capital spending, raising total borrowing from \u00a388.2m to \u00a398.2m. This was unplanned borrowing beyond the original budget, increasing debt by 11%.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Unplanned mid-year borrowing signals either capital plans changed in-year or available funding fell short. For a lender, this shows the council needing to access capital markets beyond original projections and relying on available headroom within its borrowing limits.","figures":"\u00a310m borrowed (\u00a35m HRA, \u00a35m GRA); total borrowing increased to \u00a398.212m","quote":"In March 2026 PWLB loans of \u00a310m (\u00a35m HRA and \u00a35m GRA) were drawn by the Council to fund the net unfinanced capital expenditure, leading to a total borrowing at the financial year-end of \u00a398.212m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-15","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management &amp; Prudential Indicators Annual Report 2025/26","paper_url":"https://westlancs.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s53906/53906.pdf","headline":"HRA capital programme delivered only 39% of budget: \u00a311.3m actual spend against \u00a329.3m planned","detail":"The Housing Revenue Account capital expenditure came in at \u00a311.3m against a budget of \u00a329.3m\u2014a shortfall of \u00a318m or 62%. While total council capital was \u00a319.4m against \u00a332.9m budgeted, the HRA underspend is material and warrants scrutiny of programme delivery capacity or changing investment priorities in the housing function.","category":"capital","matters":"For a lender assessing an HRA-holder, capital delivery is fundamental to the housing business plan and self-financing viability. Large underspends signal either delivery delays, capacity constraints, or changed priorities\u2014all material to risk assessment.","figures":"HRA capital: \u00a311.3m actual vs \u00a329.3m budgeted; total capital \u00a319.4m vs \u00a332.9m budgeted","quote":"HRA (applies only to housing authorities) 9,419 29,268 11,345","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-15"},"name":"West Lancashire","findings_total":63,"news_drafts_total":3}}